1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,900
Section 3

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:02,242
Emma:

3
00:00:02,242 --> 00:00:05,679
We've got to choose a topic for our experiment, haven't we, Jack?

4
00:00:05,679 --> 00:00:09,700
Were you thinking of something to do with seeds?

5
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:09,750
Jack:

6
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:11,059
That's right.

7
00:00:11,059 --> 00:00:17,400
I thought we could look at seed germination - how a seed begins to grow.

8
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:17,880
Emma:

9
00:00:17,880 --> 00:00:18,360
OK.

10
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:19,800
Any particular reason?

11
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,600
I know you're hoping to work in plant science eventually...

12
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:24,990
Jack:

13
00:00:24,990 --> 00:00:25,381
Yeah,

14
00:00:25,381 --> 00:00:29,674
but practically everything we do is going to feed into that.

15
00:00:29,674 --> 00:00:35,139
No, there's an optional module on seed structure and function in the third year

16
00:00:35,139 --> 00:00:36,700
that I might do,

17
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:40,545
so I thought it might be useful for that.

18
00:00:40,545 --> 00:00:46,100
If I choose that option, I don't have to do a dissertation module.

19
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:46,700
Emma:

20
00:00:46,700 --> 00:00:47,900
Good idea.

21
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:48,332
Jack:

22
00:00:48,332 --> 00:00:54,805
Well, I thought for this experiment we could look at the relationship between seed size

23
00:00:54,805 --> 00:00:57,826
and the way the seeds are planted.

24
00:00:57,826 --> 00:01:02,142
So, we could plant different sized seeds in different ways,

25
00:01:02,142 --> 00:01:04,300
and see which grow best.

26
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:04,685
Emma:

27
00:01:04,685 --> 00:01:05,069
OK.

28
00:01:05,069 --> 00:01:09,300
We'd need to allow time for the seeds to come up.

29
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:09,681
Jack:

30
00:01:09,681 --> 00:01:12,729
That should be fine if we start now.

31
00:01:12,729 --> 00:01:17,300
A lot of the other possible experiments need quite a bit longer.

32
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:17,713
Emma:

33
00:01:17,713 --> 00:01:21,428
So that'd make it a good one to choose.

34
00:01:21,428 --> 00:01:24,731
And I don't suppose it'd need much equipment;

35
00:01:24,731 --> 00:01:27,621
we're not doing chemical analysis or anything.

36
00:01:27,621 --> 00:01:33,400
Though that's not really an issue, we've got plenty of equipment in the laboratory.

37
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:33,760
Jack:

38
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:34,120
Yeah.

39
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:39,160
We need to have a word with the tutor if we're going to go

40
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:40,600
ahead with it though.

41
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:42,400
I'm sure our aim's OK.

42
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:48,350
It's not very ambitious but the assignment's only ten percent of our final mark,

43
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:49,200
isn't it?

44
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:54,300
But we need to be sure we're the only ones doing it.

45
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:54,739
Emma:

46
00:01:54,739 --> 00:01:57,372
Yeah, it's only five percent actually,

47
00:01:57,372 --> 00:02:02,200
but it'd be a bit boring if everyone was doing it.

48
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:02,585
Jack:

49
00:02:02,585 --> 00:02:07,200
Did you read that book on seed germination on our reading list?

50
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:07,605
Emma:

51
00:02:07,605 --> 00:02:09,224
The one by Graves?

52
00:02:09,224 --> 00:02:14,890
I looked through it for my last experiment, though it wasn't all that relevant

53
00:02:14,890 --> 00:02:15,295
there.

54
00:02:15,295 --> 00:02:18,129
It would be for this experiment, though.

55
00:02:18,129 --> 00:02:22,581
I found it quite hard to follow - lots about the theory,

56
00:02:22,581 --> 00:02:24,200
which I hadn't expected.

57
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:24,719
Jack:

58
00:02:24,719 --> 00:02:28,871
Yes, I'd been hoping for something more practical.

59
00:02:28,871 --> 00:02:35,100
It does include references to the recent findings on genetically-modified seeds, though.

60
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:35,660
Emma:

61
00:02:35,660 --> 00:02:36,220
Yes,

62
00:02:36,220 --> 00:02:37,900
that was interesting.

63
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:38,327
Jack:

64
00:02:38,327 --> 00:02:42,600
I read an article about seed germination by Lee Hall.

65
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:43,006
Emma:

66
00:02:43,006 --> 00:02:48,694
About seeds that lie in the ground for ages and only germinate after a

67
00:02:48,694 --> 00:02:49,100
fire?

68
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:49,540
Jack:

69
00:02:49,540 --> 00:02:50,862
That's the one.

70
00:02:50,862 --> 00:02:53,945
I knew a bit about it already,

71
00:02:53,945 --> 00:02:56,148
but not about this research.

72
00:02:56,148 --> 00:03:00,552
His analysis of figures comparing the times of the fires

73
00:03:00,552 --> 00:03:06,719
and the proportion of seeds that germinated was done in a lot of detail

74
00:03:06,719 --> 00:03:07,600
- very impressive.

75
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:08,050
Emma:

76
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:13,900
Was that the article with the illustrations of early stages of plant development?

77
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:15,700
They were very clear.

78
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:16,178
Jack:

79
00:03:16,178 --> 00:03:20,000
I think those diagrams were in another article.

80
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:20,332
Emma:

81
00:03:20,332 --> 00:03:24,314
Anyway, shall we have a look at the procedure for our experiment?

82
00:03:24,314 --> 00:03:27,300
We'll need to get going with it quite soon.

83
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:27,759
Jack:

84
00:03:27,759 --> 00:03:28,217
Right.

85
00:03:28,217 --> 00:03:33,720
So the first thing we have to do is find our seeds.

86
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:36,929
I think vegetable seeds would be best.

87
00:03:36,929 --> 00:03:41,056
And obviously they mustn't all be the same size.

88
00:03:41,056 --> 00:03:44,266
So, how many sorts do we need?

89
00:03:44,266 --> 00:03:46,100
About four different ones?

90
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:46,417
Emma:

91
00:03:46,417 --> 00:03:48,317
I think that would be enough.

92
00:03:48,317 --> 00:03:51,800
There'll be quite a large number of seeds for each one.

93
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:52,203
Jack:

94
00:03:52,203 --> 00:03:57,447
Then, for each seed we need to find out how much it weighs,

95
00:03:57,447 --> 00:03:59,463
and also measure its dimensions,

96
00:03:59,463 --> 00:04:03,900
and we need to keep a careful record of all that.

97
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:04,317
Emma:

98
00:04:04,317 --> 00:04:05,983
That'll be quite time-consuming.

99
00:04:05,983 --> 00:04:11,817
And we also need to decide how deep we're going to plant the seeds

100
00:04:11,817 --> 00:04:15,150
- right on the surface, a few millimetres down,

101
00:04:15,150 --> 00:04:16,400
or several centimetres.

102
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:16,824
Jack:

103
00:04:16,824 --> 00:04:17,248
OK.

104
00:04:17,248 --> 00:04:19,367
So then we get planting.

105
00:04:19,367 --> 00:04:25,300
Do you think we can plant several seeds together in the same plant pot?

106
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:25,717
Emma:

107
00:04:25,717 --> 00:04:26,133
No.

108
00:04:26,133 --> 00:04:30,300
I think we need a different one for each seed.

109
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:30,793
Jack:

110
00:04:30,793 --> 00:04:31,286
Right.

111
00:04:31,286 --> 00:04:37,200
And we'll need to label them - we can use different coloured labels.

112
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:42,365
Then we wait for the seeds to germinate - I reckon that'll be about

113
00:04:42,365 --> 00:04:45,543
three weeks, depending on what the weather's like.

114
00:04:45,543 --> 00:04:49,119
Then we see if our plants have come up,

115
00:04:49,119 --> 00:04:51,900
and write down how tall they've grown.

116
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:52,200
Emma:

117
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:55,500
Then all we have to do is look at our numbers,

118
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:57,900
and see if there's any relation between them.

119
00:04:57,900 --> 00:04:59,425
Jack:

120
00:04:59,425 --> 00:05:02,475
That's right.

121
00:05:02,475 --> 00:05:04,000
So ...
